Transition states in language models have proven beneficial, but their utility is limited when working with code-mixed languages that incorporate transition positions from multiple languages. Research on these languages is currently hindered by a lack of large-scale training data. To overcome this challenge, we've developed a novel approach that automatically identifies essential transition points between languages in code-mixed text. A specially designed autoencoder, utilizing relative positional representation, is then adapted to learn language-specific dynamics based on these transition points. Experimental results on the Wikipedia2019 dataset demonstrate our method's effectiveness, with significant improvements in NLP tasks such as sentiment analysis and question answering. Our work not only resolves issues specific to NER and entity linking tasks and related research but also paves the way for further applications in transition-based tasks. Our method's open-source code can be accessed at https://github.com/jmeckdo/PESTO. 
