This study develops an innovative, interactive approach to analyzing large visual datasets by integrating the power of natural language processing, utilizing Word2Vec embeddings and Ontodia, a platform for creating and managing knowledge graphs based on Wikidata. The research identifies key categories within datasets and generates human-friendly summaries through a combination of word embeddings, human assessment, and visualizations. We demonstrate the efficacy of this method in four distinct datasets focusing on visual representations, spinal cord anatomy, and specific Wikidata categories. The results reveal that word embeddings accurately capture essential characteristics, aligning with human observations, and also enable the discovery of hidden insights not previously recognized. Furthermore, we showcase the applicability of this technique in exploring complex data sets and highlight its potential for contributions to the broader AI and knowledge graph communities. The developed methods and findings are further detailed on a dedicated public platform. 

